TI  - Graph theory method for efficient routing in            wireless sensor networks (WSNs) using connected            dominating set (CDS) for real-time monitoring in            diverse fields e.g. environmental sensing fields,            involves providing structured and energy-efficient way            to route data when reducing overhead
AB  - 
                   NOVELTY - The method involves providing a structured and                energy-efficient way to route data while reducing                overhead using a CDS for routing in WSNs. The                approach ensures that the network remains                connected, efficient, and resilient even in the                face of network dynamics in real-time applications,                such as disaster recovery scenarios. Only nodes in                the CDS (B, F, L) are responsible for forwarding                data packets. The Non-CDS nodes (A, C, G) send data                to the nearest CDS node (B, F, L). The overall                energy consumption of the network is reduced as                only a small subset of the CDS nodes are actively                involved in routing. The CDS-based routing enables                reliable communication with reduced energy                consumption, which is critical in the context of                limited resources in a disaster scenario by                selecting a small subset of nodes to form a virtual                backbone.
                    USE - Graph theory method for efficient routing in                wireless sensor networks (WSNs) using connected                dominating set (CDS) for real-time monitoring in                diverse fields such as environmental sensing                fields, smart cities, industrial automation field,                military applications and disaster recovery                scenario such as wildfire, earthquake or                flood.
                    ADVANTAGE - The control message overhead is reduced,                leading to more efficient use of bandwidth by                limiting routing to a smaller subset of nodes. The                energy consumption is significantly reduced,                leading to a longer network lifetime since fewer                nodes participate in the communication. The network                maintains efficiency even in large-scale                environments by dynamically updating the CDS as                nodes move or fail. The network overall lifetime is                maximized, by using a rotating CDS approach where                different nodes take turns being part of the                CDS.
